{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Warm-path measurement, and why the design changed\n\nThe original plan specified **one stat per event file** for the catch-up diff. Measured against the real store — 9,875 tickets, 141,701 event files — that cannot meet this ticket's own 500 ms warm budget. Three call shapes, same store:\n\n| Approach | Warm catch-up |\n|---|---|\n| `listdir` + per-file `stat` | 4,850 ms |\n| `os.scandir` + `entry.stat()` | 2,400 ms |\n| stat the ticket **directory** only | 51 ms |\n\nThe cost is the **event-file count**, not the call shape: optimizing the syscall still lands ~5x over budget. Only a per-directory stat fits.\n\nA note on how this could have been hidden: the AC measures a *synthetic* 10,000-ticket store, which holds roughly 1 event per ticket. Per-file statting would have passed that test at ~250 ms while taking 2.4 s on the real store. Passing by being measured on something easier than reality is not passing, so the design changed instead of the criterion.\n\n### Implemented result\n\nTwo-level diff, directory-mtime pre-filter, measured on the same 9,875-ticket store:\n\n- Cold build: 54.7 s\n- **Warm catch-up p95: 111.4 ms over 20 runs** (min 89.3, max 519.7)\n- End-to-end `find_similar`: 120–208 ms, including open + catch-up + query\n\nThat is 4.5x inside the 500 ms budget, against 4,360 ms before.\n\n### Why the mtime pre-filter is sound, and its exact hole\n\nA directory's mtime changes when an entry inside it is added, removed or renamed. Those are the **only** ways rebar mutates a ticket: events are append-only files, and retirement is a rename. So an unchanged mtime implies unchanged event set.\n\nThe residual hole is an **in-place rewrite of an existing event file that leaves the directory mtime untouched**. rebar never does this. Stating it rather than hiding it: if rebar ever gains an in-place event rewrite, this pre-filter becomes unsound and must be revisited.\n\nTwo properties keep the failure direction safe:\n\n1. **The hash decides, not the mtime.** A moved mtime only *permits* the exact `compute_dir_hash` check; the hash is what triggers a re-index. A spurious mtime bump costs redundant hashing, not a wrong result.\n2. **The mtime is read before the content.** A write landing between the two reads makes the stored mtime look stale on the next run, so the ticket is re-hashed. The failure mode is redundant work, never a missed change.\n\n### Index version\n\n`INDEX_VERSION` 1 → 2, since `dirstate` gained `dir_mtime_ns`. An index written by the previous version is detected as stale, warned about, discarded and rebuilt — verified on the real store.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793585466950591416,"uuid":"4b34a93c-e272-4bf0-82d4-9b87285c8788"}